From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christophe Pettus <xof@thebuild.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 00:10:42 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 22/22] H13: cap image decodes by declared and codec-reported
 pixel count

An image's decoders allocate in proportion to its size, so a few bytes
can demand gigabytes when a renderer asks for its pixels. The per-stream
byte limit does not help: the image codecs are outside it.

DecodeLimits gains max_image_pixels (default None, no limit), checked
wherever an image's size first becomes known, before the allocation it
governs. Every refusal is the new DecodeFailure::ImagePixelLimit and
one bump of the thread-local image_pixel_limit_hits().

- Declared: before decryption and any filter, the width x height the
  image parameters declare. Non-image decodes declare 0 x 0 and are
  never refused.
- Codec header: CCITT, JBIG2 and JPEG 2000 size their output from the
  encoded data, not the dictionary. Filter::apply passes the cap down:
  CCITT checks /Columns x rows (4 rows per byte when /Rows is 0, the
  most Group 3 can decode), JBIG2 the page size after reading segment
  headers, JPEG 2000 the full-resolution size from a header-only parse
  (its working set ignores the requested resolution), and DCT the frame
  size after decode_headers().
- Reported: after the filter loop, the size the codec reports, which
  is what hayro-interpret converts at (CCITT mixes /Columns with the
  declared height).

Inline images are parsed without their document and so carried no
limits; the new Stream::decoded_inline_image holds them to a given
document's pixel cap, and the interpreter uses it for every inline
image it draws. In hayro-interpret a refused image raises the new
InterpreterWarning::ImagePixelLimit instead of ImageDecodeFailure, so it
is reported once, as what it is.

Core opens every document with Limits.max_image_pixels in its decode
limits, and the per-page signal window samples the new counter alongside
the interpreter's warning.
